\section*{Problem 1 - 141011}

Someone chooses a natural number \(n\), adds the natural numbers from 1 to \(n\) together, and obtains the sum \(1+2+\ldots+n\) as a three-digit number, which (like 777) consists entirely of the same digits.

Determine all possibilities for choosing a number \(n\) for which this is true!

Given a positive integer $n$, there are $3 n$ numbers satisfying:
$$
\begin{array}{c}
0 \leqslant a_{1} \leqslant a_{2} \leqslant \cdots \leqslant a_{3 n}, \\
\text { and }\left(\sum_{i=1}^{3 n} a_{i}\right)^{3} \geqslant k_{n}\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n} a_{i} a_{n+i} a_{2 n+i}\right) \text { always holds. }
\end{array}
$$

Find the best possible value of $k_{n}$ (expressed in terms of $n$).

